Arguments as supplied: `$ARGUMENTS`

Parsing rule:
- First token: the plan path.
- Remainder: `crew NAME` (Form 1) or `auto` (Form 2; default `auto` when absent or empty).
- OpenCode hands the whole message over as one quoted string: strip one pair of surrounding quotes before tokenizing.

Supervise the execution of one plan through the lee-llm-router staffing
router. This command has exactly two argument forms, chosen from the
bound arguments:

1. `/supervise <plan-path> crew NAME` (Codex: `/prompts:supervise <plan-path> crew NAME`) — staff every packet with the saved
   crew `NAME` via `staff --mode crew NAME`.
2. `/supervise <plan-path> auto` (Codex: `/prompts:supervise <plan-path> auto`) — staff every packet in auto mode from the
   conservative packet-class derivation. If the second argument is omitted,
   use this form.

## Dispatch pattern

1. Start `lee-llm-router run …` detached: `setsid nohup bash -c "cd <repo> && lee-llm-router run … > <log> 2>&1; echo done=\$? >> <log>" < /dev/null &`.
2. Poll in bounded foreground loops of at most 90 s each: `timeout 90 bash -c "until grep -q '^done=' <log>; do sleep 10; done"`; repeat the poll call until it succeeds; never a single call longer than 90 s; never a background tool; never end the turn while the log lacks `done=`. If a poll shows the log without `done=` past the packet's bound plus 5 minutes, run `lee-llm-router census --json` and report the live run instead of waiting further (the router's own kill will land; the supervisor never kills processes itself).
3. Then read the attempt record `run` printed (or `census`/the ledger tail) before deciding.

Same pattern for reviews. Never end your turn while anything you started is running.

## Hard command boundary

The numbered loop below may call only these six router commands:

- `lee-llm-router staff`
- `lee-llm-router run`
- `lee-llm-router classify-failure`
- `lee-llm-router next-action`
- `lee-llm-router census`
- `lee-llm-router evidence rollup`

It must not invoke providers directly: never run `claude`, `codex`, `agy`,
`opencode`, `omp`, `pi`, or any other provider binary, never construct a
provider dispatch outside `lee-llm-router run`, and never bypass an
exclusion or the never-automatic policy. Every worker and reviewer dispatch
goes through `lee-llm-router run`, which registers the run and writes its
schema-v2 attempt record to the per-host ledger.

## Numbered execution loop

1. **Read the plan.** Read the plan at the bound plan path in full before any
   other action. Use only facts the plan establishes; never fabricate task
   facts. If the path is missing, inspect recoverable path/setup defects within
   scope; proceed only with a uniquely verifiable intended source. Ambiguous
   consequential intent requires a concrete clarification; never invent a plan.
   Reconcile current conversation authority, waivers, exact pins, review gates
   and inherited parent limits before staffing. Missing numeric caps alone
   do not require an ask or authorize an invented budget.

2. **Packetize under Rule B.** Split the plan into small bounded work
   packets. Every packet states: objective; exact files or resources owned;
   forbidden files or resources; expected artifact and where to write it;
   required evidence; stop/escalation condition. Every packet has exact
   owned paths and a finite oracle: a named test/validator command, or a
   named reviewer, never "it looks right". Every packet declares a
   runtime bound in minutes next to its oracle; a packet without one is not
   dispatchable. A packet that cannot state its owned-file list in one line
   is too big — split it into multiple packets.

3. **Confirm a clear mutation surface.** Run exactly:

       lee-llm-router census --json

   Parallel work is permitted only for disjoint owned paths: write the
   owned-path list per packet and confirm the intersection is empty before
   any parallel dispatch. If a live run already owns intersecting paths,
   wait for it to finish or shrink the packet; never co-own paths.

4. **Derive the class per packet.** Write each packet as a Markdown file
   and run exactly:

       lee-llm-router staff --from-packet <packet-path> --json

   Save each JSON result as that packet's class-derivation evidence. The
   derivation is conservative: language comes from owned-path extensions
   (`mixed` for multiple); size comes from declared file/line estimates and
   is `l` if undeclared; the oracle is `deterministic` only for a named
   test/validator, else `judge` for a named reviewer, else `none`; domain
   tags are only explicit `classes.yaml` keyword-table matches; the role
   comes from the packet kind. Overrides are recorded on the attempt; never
   invent them.

5. **Staff each packet as requested.** Form 1 — run exactly:

       lee-llm-router staff --mode crew <name> --json

   Form 2 — run exactly:

       lee-llm-router staff --mode auto --role <role> --class <class> --json

   filling `<role>` and `<class>` from the derivation. The two forms name
   the chosen route and its same-role fallback ladder under different
   JSON keys — read the one that matches the form you ran:

   - Form 1 (crew): the chosen route is `worker_routes.<role>[0]`; the
     same-role fallback ladder is the remaining entries,
     `worker_routes.<role>[1:]`, in order. Ignore the top-level
     `escalation`/`escalation_ladder` fields — they are not role-scoped and
     do not name the fallback ladder for every role.
   - Form 2 (auto): the chosen route is `selected_route`; the same-role
     fallback ladder is `escalation`, in order.

   Either way, also read the block's selection basis, reason, exclusions,
   and reviewer disclosures before dispatching.

   A user-named worker route that is not the selected route in a saved crew
   does not erase the fallback ladder. First obtain the auto block for the
   derived role/class and retain its same-role `escalation` list. Record the
   user's exact primary-route override and authority separately; check the
   primary against the same eligibility path at dispatch. Remove that primary
   from the fallback list if present. If no eligible fallback remains, stop
   before dispatch and report the staffing gap. A manual primary is one
   authorized start plus at most one narrowed same-route repair, not a
   permanent same-route pin.

6. **Dispatch the packet with the block's chosen route.** Run exactly:

       lee-llm-router run --role <role> --class <class> --packet <packet-path> --route <chosen-route-id> --supervisor-route <supervisor-route-id> --owned-paths <owned-path> [--owned-paths <owned-path> ...] --class-derivation <derivation-json-path> --oracle <oracle-cmd> --timeout <bound×60> --stall-minutes 10 --progress-minutes 20 --json

   `--route` is exactly step 5's chosen route, or the separately recorded
   user-authorized primary override for its first start — never omitted.
   `run` never re-selects inside a
   supervised loop: an omitted `--route` lets `run` auto-select its own
   cheapest-eligible route, silently discarding the crew or auto-mode
   choice the block just made. `--supervisor-route` attests your own route
   identity. `--owned-paths` repeats every exact path this packet owns —
   nothing more. `--class-derivation` carries the step-4 evidence. Pass
   `--oracle` with the packet's finite oracle whenever it is a
   deterministic command; for a judge-oracle packet the named reviewer is
   step 11. The `run` command always carries `--timeout <bound×60>`,
   `--stall-minutes 10`, `--progress-minutes 20` (or the packet's own
   smaller values), never omitted: activity is not progress; a worker that
   streams without touching its owned files is killed by the router, not by
   the supervisor noticing. Dispatch parallel packets only when their owned
   paths are disjoint.

   If `run` refuses the dispatch on eligibility (exit 3) because the
   chosen route became ineligible between staffing and dispatch, do not
   re-run `staff` and do not let `run` auto-select: take the next route in
   step 5's same-role fallback ladder, record the fallback route and the
   exact refusal reason on the attempt, and dispatch again with `--route`
   set to that fallback. Repeat in ladder order only while `run` refuses on
   eligibility (exit 3); stop and escalate per step 9 if the ladder is
   exhausted. Any other nonzero exit (not 0, not 3) is not an eligibility
   refusal — stop the fallback loop immediately and escalate per step 9
   rather than treating it as a reason to keep trying the ladder.

7. **Personally verify the oracle.** A worker's summary is never evidence.
   Run the oracle yourself, read the full candidate diff (`git status
   --short`, `git diff --stat`, `git diff`), and inspect the artifacts.
   Only evidence you generated or read directly can support acceptance.

8. **Classify every failure.** For a failed or unverified attempt run
   exactly:

       lee-llm-router classify-failure --record <attempt-record-path> --json

   then exactly:

       lee-llm-router next-action --input <classify-json-path> --repair-count <completed-same-route-repairs>

   Pass the actual completed same-route repair count from the parent record:
   0 on first rejection; 1 after the failed repair. `supervisor_judgment`
   and `return_to_planner` belong to the supervising AI: inspect the evidence
   and perform bounded diagnosis/planning inside existing authority. They
   are not automatic requests to Lee. Mapping advice never overrides the
   parent method prohibition, accounting or remaining limits.

   `spec_rejected` and `capability_rejected` enter only through an explicit
   `--judgment` after your review; never infer them from prose. A
   `platform_timeout` whose provenance note says `stall` or `no_progress`
   is a *hung or looping worker*, not slowness: the supervisor
   never re-dispatches the same packet unchanged. Step 9's single repair for
   this case is one of: halve the packet, or tighten the owned paths/oracle,
   or pass a smaller `--timeout`; then dispatch once; if it times out
   again for any kind, escalate up the ladder immediately.

   Count every started worker that fails to return a verified candidate,
   including claim collisions, stalls, ceiling timeouts, and attempts whose
   ledger append fails. A missing record is an accounting/control failure:
   preserve its command, timestamps, log and exit evidence, stop that packet's
   retry loop, and repair the ledger path before another dispatch. Do not
   relabel operational noncompletion as cognitive inability; it still counts
   against route reliability.

9. **Repair once, then escalate.** Remediation follows `next-action`: one
   same-route capability repair precedes escalation. If that repair fails,
   escalate using the staffing block's ladder, reviewer, and authority
   disclosures: stop the worker, audit the actual repository state,
   preserve useful work, write a handoff of verified facts only, and move
   up a tier. A replacement inherits facts, not the failed worker's story.
   Across parallel packets in the same transaction, two terminal
   noncompletions (as counted in step 8) on one route require re-staffing:
   exclude that route from the saved same-role fallback ladder for the rest
   of this transaction, even if a fresh auto block selects it again. A new
   user authorization is required to restore it. Step 6's no-re-staff rule
   applies only to a single exit-3 eligibility-refusal loop, not to this
   post-failure decision. After one narrowed same-route repair fails within a stable
   packet, dispatch the next eligible same-role route with `--parent` and
   `--escalation-reason`. The supervisor may make an emergency bounded edit
   only if no eligible worker can be dispatched; record why, then obtain the
   required independent review. Reviewer-directed repair is still author
   work and does not exempt the worker escalation decision.

10. **Ledger every attempt.** Every dispatch — work, repair, escalation,
    review — is an attempt record in the per-host ledger. Never edit or
    discard ledger lines; cite exact attempt ids in the final report.
    Summarize with:

        lee-llm-router evidence rollup

11. **Independent review.** For every candidate you intend to accept,
    find the review route the same way step 5 found the worker route, from
    the same form you are running:

    - Form 1 (crew): reuse step 5's crew block for this packet — one
      `staff --mode crew <name> --json` call already names every role's
      routes. The review route is `worker_routes.review[0]`; its same-role
      fallback ladder is `worker_routes.review[1:]`. Crew bindings are a
      fixed, pre-authorized declaration, so there is no `--author-route`
      exclusion to apply here.
    - Form 2 (auto): run
      `staff --mode auto --role review --class <review-class>
      --author-route <worker-route-id> --json` (excluding the worker's own
      route as the author so review independence is evaluated), and read
      that block's `selected_route` and `escalation` exactly as in step 5.

    Then dispatch exactly:

        lee-llm-router run --role review --class <review-class> --packet <review-packet-path> --route <review-route-id> --author-route <worker-route-id> --supervisor-route <supervisor-route-id> --owned-paths <owned-path> [--owned-paths <owned-path> ...] --timeout <review-bound×60> --stall-minutes 10 --progress-minutes 0 --json

   A review is bounded like any worker: the review packet declares its own runtime bound;
   `--progress-minutes 0` because a reviewer legitimately writes no owned file, so only the
   stall bound and the ceiling apply to it.

    `--route` is exactly the review route found above — the review
    dispatch never re-selects inside a supervised loop either, and follows
    the same same-role-ladder-only fallback discipline as step 6 if `run`
    refuses it on eligibility (exit 3). Classify its findings:
    contract-blocking defects, non-blocking hardening, or future concerns;
    blocking findings cite the violated requirement and a reproducer.
    Re-derive any classification that would drive a change; never feed
    review labels straight into remediation.

12. **Inspect every diff.** After every worker run that had repository
    access, compare what changed against the packet's owned paths —
    including untracked files. A worker that leaves any file outside its
    owned paths has violated scope, however harmless.

13. **Complete one bounded review transaction.** Author, independent review,
    up to two reviewer-directed remediation rounds, then final independent
    review of the final material candidate. All packets, children, alternate
    methods, reviewers, probes and resumed sessions consume the same parent
    allowance and supplied envelope. Stop sooner at acceptance or economic
    nonconvergence; two rounds are a ceiling, not a quota or acceptance.
    Required correctness/security gates and 0 High / 0 Medium still apply
    after supervisor triage against the frozen DoD. No third author repair
    round or successor transaction silently renews authority. If final review
    still blocks, preserve the best candidate and report truthful incomplete
    state. Never end while anything you started remains running.

14. **Report.** Finish with the staffing block for every packet plus exact
    ledger evidence: attempt ids, routes, selection basis and reason, usage
    basis, verdicts, and next actions — copied from the ledger records,
    not narrated. The ledger, not its narrative, proves every attempt.

## Genuine stops (D209)

Do not stop at packet boundaries or while a worker is running. A genuine
stop requires an authority boundary, unresolved contradiction, missing
credential, material irreversible risk, or an external dependency that
makes useful progress impossible, after reconciling D204–D213. Also stop
truthfully incomplete at a supplied execution limit or economic nonconvergence.
Before a task stop, reassess permitted alternatives and preserve useful state;
stop the failed method promptly, continue useful authorized recovery or disjoint
work, and stop the task only when no useful permitted path remains. A definitive
capability/permission denial permits zero equivalent-syntax retries. An already
authorized alternative interactive capability may satisfy the unchanged DoD;
verify the grant and use it without ritual continuation approval.

## Parent acceptance and method record — superseding contract 2026-09-29

This dated contract supersedes indefinite convergence and the *exclusive* stop
condition in the historical Rule J quotation below. Retain that quotation as
history; apply this bounded contract on the supported consuming path.

Use the existing parent WORK/STATE artifact, one stable identity and one
supervisor-owned ledger inherited by children and resumed sessions. Before the
first substantive start freeze the DoD, minimum useful result, early value
checkpoint, required actual consuming journey, and a required independent-review
row (kind: independent_review). Freeze the entire gap ID/proposition/source set
at inception; findings map to those rows, never create fresh method history or
unrelated scope. Governing scope changes require attributed authority and a
recorded handoff retaining counters; this transaction cannot silently widen. Run the smallest actual
journey early and again for final acceptance. Green leaves, proxy tests, logs,
file edits and review text alone do not prove parent delivery.

Each acceptance row records stable requirement ID, precise proposition and
frozen source; OPEN, BLOCKED, PROVED, WAIVED or NOT_REQUIRED; inspected evidence,
observation time and candidate revision/hash; remaining gap and next finite
falsifier; scoped authority source/conditions; and retained transition history,
attempts, methods and invalidated proofs. BLOCKED names the real dependency,
not necessarily a Lee decision. PROVED needs the row's actual oracle, including
required human/consuming evidence. WAIVED and NOT_REQUIRED are never PROVED.
Respect a task waiver's scope; preserve final-release gates. Resume reconciles
current authority. A stale reviewer cannot resurrect a waiver; governing
changes require attributed authority and a history transition. Material changes
reopen affected proofs only, retaining old evidence.

Before every substantive author/reviewer/repair/planner/diagnostic dispatch,
name OPEN/BLOCKED target rows, the proposition, expected evidence, finite oracle
and falsifier, bound, causal method (capability path, hypothesis and known
limitation), and why this action could advance the gap. A review targets the frozen
independent-review gap and may also target a reopened affected proof. Keep that
review row OPEN until final material source is reviewed; reopen it with retained
evidence/history whenever material repairs invalidate its coverage. With no relevant gap, do
not dispatch engineering/review; closure/accounting/shutdown stay bounded.
Unknown failure can justify one finite diagnostic question, not generic churn.

At every failure, blocking review, missed value checkpoint, or proposed stop,
record owed parent outcome and revision/best artifact; evidence of movement;
failure hypothesis, alternatives considered, next action and falsifier/bound;
cumulative starts (including failures, reviews, children and probes), directed
repair rounds and resources/remaining authority; and disposition. Keep usage
reported/calculated/unavailable distinct; log pre-provider refusals separately.
Unavailable start/time accounting blocks a new start until reconciled; unavailable token
usage stays unavailable. This is supervisor work, not a Lee paperwork gate.

Method history follows the gap and causal capability/hypothesis, never worker,
packet/hash, route or finding ID. Two consecutive evidence-bearing attempts or
checkpoints without meaningful proposition movement prohibit that method
unchanged. Definitive denial prohibits it immediately. Renaming is no change;
require a concrete authorized causal alternative, expected result and finite
bound. Discovery eliminating an unworkable path counts once. Repeated edits,
tests or alternating blockers with no net advance do not reset value time.
Interactive approval/recall setup can be intermediate movement while full recall
remains OPEN. Retain first useful result time, last meaningful movement across
resume, earned criteria, offsetting regressions and next checkpoint. Remaining
quota never justifies useless execution; progress never exceeds a supplied cap.

Version-1 mechanical admission on new supervised starts uses existing `run`:
append `--unit-id <stable-parent> --unit-state <absolute-parent-STATE.jsonl>
--unit-decision <decision.json>` to **every** worker/reviewer/child start. Read
the pinned router source's `docs/staffing/supervise-parent-admission.md` for
schema/examples; locate that source if the consuming workspace lacks the doc.
The decision JSON carries `version: 1`, `unit_id`, actual `completed_starts`,
`previous` (null initially, then SHA256 of the last journal decision encoded by
`json.dumps(decision, sort_keys=True).encode()`), unchanged `limits` (empty when
none supplied), the authoritative `gaps`, and one retained `assessments` entry
per start (`start`, boolean `advanced`, boolean `denied`, inspected `evidence`).
`dispatch` names `targets`, stable causal `method`, `capability`, `hypothesis`,
`proposition`, `oracle`, `falsifier`, `alternatives`, `why`, `revision`, `phase`
(author/diagnose/repair/review/final_review), and cumulative `repair_round` 0–2.
The existing finite `--timeout` is its bound. Keep the
journal path constant through resume; do not truncate, copy a fresh journal or
reset counts. Legacy runs remain compatible, but are not this opted-in contract.
Version 1 serializes opted-in starts within one parent, including disjoint
packets: finish/reconcile and assess each before the next start. Earlier parallel
instructions and Rules H/I describe legacy or genuinely independent authorized
work. Do not split this parent into new units or omit flags to bypass inherited
accounting. Schedule its disjoint useful work serially within the same grant.
This seam enforces continuity, accounting, two-round allowance, recorded method
prohibitions and supplied limits before provider launch; it cannot verify the
truth of progress narratives. Review after repair closes that directed round;
a further blocking review requires the next round, not reuse of round 1. Multiple
repair packets before that review share the active round. After round 2 only
final review remains; no review/diagnosis cycling or author restart. A failed
review execution permits one finite recovery, retaining consumed starts and
unknown outcome. If initial review covers unchanged final source, close without
a duplicate review. Required final acceptance evidence is never inferred from
phase labels or attempt records. Reserve final verification/shutdown capacity when
an outer cap is supplied. No supplied numeric cap means economical checkpoints,
not a fabricated Lee budget. Record actual admission refusals in WORK.

For a genuine human escalation, name: exact decision/action only Lee can take;
why this gap requires it now; permitted alternatives considered/tried and why
insufficient; recommendation with evidence; consequences for outcome, scope,
risk and resources. No "May I continue?" inside an existing grant. Credentials,
consent and real human acceptance remain human actions. Do not widen a product
promise or irreversible risk. Continue independent useful authorized work while
that action is pending. Economic stops report incomplete state and business
choices without requesting an operational rubber stamp.

At closure required applicable rows must be PROVED, with authorized WAIVED or
NOT_REQUIRED rows distinguished, required independent review and parent journey
satisfied. Router success and completed children do not establish acceptance.

## Operating rules (D209)

- **Usage truth.** No usage is invented. Provider-reported, observed,
  calculated, and unavailable facts stay distinct; subscription equivalents
  never count as metered spend.
- **Findings classification.** The supervisor reads every candidate diff
  and classifies review findings as contract-blocking defects, non-blocking
  hardening, or future concerns. Blocking findings cite the violated
  requirement and a reproducer.
- **Rule B packet shape.** Every packet states: objective; exact files or
  resources owned; forbidden files or resources; expected artifact and
  where to write it; required evidence; stop/escalation condition.
- **Disjoint parallelism.** Concurrent packets must have disjoint owned
  paths. Maintain exactly one authoritative integration owner: the
  supervisor.
- **No-invented-policy.** Exclusions and never-automatic policy are never
  bypassed; selection is `explicit` or `explain_cheapest_eligible`; never
  invent a policy the staffing block does not state, and never report usage
  the ledger did not record.

## Doctrine guardrails (quoted verbatim)

Source: governed-execution-engine-docs,
`docs/supervised-agent-operating-doctrine.md`. Each guardrail below is
quoted exactly, not paraphrased, and labeled with its rule.

- **Rule A — Agent confinement is verified, not instructed.** "A prompt is a request, not a sandbox."
- **Rule B — Prefer small bounded work packets.** "A packet that cannot state its owned-file list in one line is too big."
- **Rule C — Stop and escalate on spec deviation.** "Do not silently improvise an alternative architecture."
- **Rule D — The supervisor reviews evidence, not worker summaries.** "Worker summaries are consistently more confident than the underlying work."
- **Rule E — Staffing ladder (empirical, not a model ranking).** "Before assigning, ask: *what oracle proves this correct?*"
- **Rule F — Escalation triggers, and what to do at them.** "Escalate the tier on evidence, not on a fixed defect count."
- **Rule G — A replacement worker inherits facts, not the failed worker's story.** "A stalled worker's narrative is the least reliable artifact in the system: it describes intent, at the moment the work stopped being trustworthy."
- **Rule H — Parallelism requires disjoint mutation ownership.** "Two agents holding the same mutable file produce a diff no one can attribute, and a candidate no one can freeze."
- **Rule I — Read-only research parallelises well; its conclusions still need judgment.** "Fan-out is cheap and effective for *finding* things and unreliable for *classifying* them."
- **Rule J — Review until convergence; "more findings" is not a stopping condition.** "The supervisor stops as genuinely blocked only if the same release blocker survives two consecutive evidence-backed remediation attempts without material progress."
- **Rule K — A failed cheap-worker execution is not a failed architecture.** "Worker capability, implementation quality, and architecture validity are three separate hypotheses."
- "Activity ≠ progress. A hung worker is terminated by the router within its stall bound; a supervisor that waits past the bound is the loop."

This command supervises through the router only. It never dispatches a
provider directly, never reports usage the ledger did not record, and ends
only with the staffing blocks and ledger evidence above.

Review assessments include review_outcome: passed, blocking or incomplete,
with inspected evidence. A record proves accounting, never review success.
Incomplete execution permits one bounded recovery within the same round; a
completed blocking review requires the next directed repair round. Multiple
targets name advanced_targets explicitly; movement on another row never resets
the failed target’s method streak. Labels/capability claims remain subject to
independent evidence inspection.

## Enforced economical checkpoint — 2026-10-01

Before the next opted-in parent dispatch, freeze a finite internal execution checkpoint in the decision as `checkpoint: {version: 1, max_starts: <absolute cumulative ceiling>, max_seconds: <absolute cumulative routed-seconds ceiling>, source: <attributed checkpoint basis>}`. Include every retained start, reconciliation, child, review and owner handoff when calculating ceilings. This is economical supervisor judgment, not an invented user budget; supplied outer limits also remain binding. Once adopted, the router refuses removing or changing this checkpoint, and refuses the next reservation before provider launch when either ceiling is exhausted. Cloned child decisions must retain it. Do not use an unadopted legacy start or new parent to evade it.

Choose a proportionate bounded checkpoint that reserves independent review and consuming verification. A model, method, packet, workstream or local test improvement cannot renew the checkpoint. Report acceptance-gap movement separately from implementation evidence: if owner acceptance remains unproved, local fixes do not justify an unlimited new sequence. At exhaustion preserve useful state and return an incomplete disposition; do not request routine permission or claim the task is complete. Explicit later budget authority requires its own supported contract, not editing the frozen checkpoint.

D268 permits one small same-DoD internal checkpoint extension. Keep the original checkpoint immutable; a separate `checkpoint_extension: {version: 1, starts: 1, source: <attributed exception and evidence>}` grants at most one extra cumulative start and no extra time. Once adopted it cannot be changed or removed. It never raises supplied outer limits and never renews through a successor. Do not use this once-only allowance for an unchanged failed method.
